    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "Cascaded assignments with tuple targets do not follow Python's order of assignment:\n\n```python\ndef no_star():\n    d = [0, 0]\n    d[0], d[1] = d[1], d[0] = 1, 2\n    return d          # Python [2, 1], Cython master [2, 2]\n\ndef whole_target():\n    d = [0, 0]\n    d[0], d[1] = d = 1, 2\n    return d          # Python (1, 2), Cython master: TypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\n\ndef empty_star():\n    d = [0, 0]\n    d[0], d[1] = d[1], d[0], *rest = 1, 2\n    return d, rest    # Python ([2, 1], []), Cython master ([1, 2], [])\n```\n\nPython assigns one complete target after the other, from left to right. `flatten_parallel_assignments` assigns the plain targets of the cascade first and then the tuple targets position by position, which gives the first two results. For the third, the branch I added in GH-8105 for an empty starred target emits that target before the others. Before GH-8105 that line crashed the compiler.\n\nHanding such cascades to the runtime unpacking would follow Python's order, as long as the compile-time length checks still run for every target, so that errors such as `a, b, *r = x, y, z = 1, 2` are still reported. How would you like this handled?\n\nCython master (2a4662089), Python 3.14, Windows.\n\nAI disclosure: Codex (GPT-6.1 Sol) found this. I used Claude Code (Claude Opus 5.5) to write the reproducers and this text, and Codex (GPT-6.1 Sol) and a second Claude Code session to review them. I read it before posting.\n",
